# NOTICE

`libwebgrep` is an original implementation written in pure Zig.

## Algorithmic References & Attributions

1. **Byte Frequency Heuristic (`src/finder/frequency.zig`)**
   The byte frequency rank table is hand-authored based on English prose and source code token distributions. Callers may supply a custom `[256]u8` rank table via `FindOptions.frequency`.

2. **SIMD Packed-Pair Substring Filtering (`src/finder/packed_pair.zig`)**
   Literals up to 32 bytes use generic SIMD paired-byte filtering: two rare anchor offsets are loaded into vectors, compared in parallel, and bitwise-ANDed before full confirmation. This approach is based on Wojciech Muła's article *"SIMD-friendly algorithms for substring searching"* (2016-11-28, http://0x80.pl/articles/simd-strfind.html). Anchor selection uses our frequency rank table rather than fixed first/last bytes. For patterns longer than 32 bytes, adaptive fallback to Two-Way is triggered when at least 50 candidate verifications occur with an average skip below 8 bytes.

3. **Two-Way String Matching (`src/finder/two_way.zig`)**
   Implemented from the algorithm published by Crochemore and Perrin:
   > Maxime Crochemore and Dominique Perrin, "Two-way string-matching", *Journal of the ACM*, 38(3):651–675, 1991.

4. **Work-Stealing Deque (`src/walk/deque.zig`)**
   Implemented from the dynamic circular work-stealing deque paper:
   > David Chase and Yossi Lev, "Dynamic Circular Work-Stealing Deque", *SPAA '05*, pages 21–28, 2005. https://doi.org/10.1145/1073970.1073974

5. **Aho-Corasick Automaton (`src/finder/aho.zig`)**
   Implemented from the failure-function automaton published by Aho and Corasick:
   > Alfred V. Aho and Margaret J. Corasick, "Efficient string matching: an aid to bibliographic search", *Communications of the ACM*, 18(6):333–340, 1975.

6. **Slim Teddy Nibble-Mask SIMD Engine (`src/finder/teddy.zig`)**
   The nibble-lookup SIMD multi-pattern prefilter (`vtbl` on NEON, `pshufb` on SSSE3, `vpshufb` on AVX2) is implemented from the public descriptions by Geoff Langdale (https://branchfree.org/2024/06/29/hyperscan-papers-harry-and-teddy-simd-literal-matchers/) and the paper:
   > Kun Qiu, Harry Chang, Yang Hong, Wenjun Zhu, Xiang Wang, and Baoqian Li, "Teddy: An Efficient SIMD-based Literal Matching Engine for Scalable Deep Packet Inspection", *ICPP '21*, 2021. https://doi.org/10.1145/3472456.3473512

7. **Thompson NFA / PikeVM Regular Expression Engine (`src/regex.zig`)**
   Linear-time lockstep Thompson NFA simulation is based on Ken Thompson's 1968 CACM paper (*"Programming Techniques: Regular expression search algorithm"*) and Russ Cox's *"Regular Expression Matching: the Virtual Machine Approach"* (https://swtch.com/~rsc/regexp/regexp2.html).
